Tests of the hi-res render path
===============================

The tests of the hi-res fork (docs/hires/DESIGN.md, section 6). The unit tests
and the present test use synthetic data only; the game-data scripts read a
Black Gate installation at run time and never write into it. Nothing derived
from the game's art is committed: goldens are hash lists only.

Layout
------

  doctest.h        the test framework (vendored, see below)
  unit/            hires_unit: SDL-free unit tests
    main.cc          entry point; data_path() for tests/data
    test_support.h   deterministic PRNG (SplitMix64) and FNV-1a-64
    ibuf_ops.h/.cc   a deterministic stream of Image_buffer8 operations
    test_flat_source.cc
                     pins the fill rule of the flat layer
                     (objs/flat_source.h) to the original loop, quirks and
                     frame-load order included
    test_ibuf_golden.cc
                     pins the scale-1 behaviour of Image_buffer8 against
                     data/ibuf_golden.txt (invariant I2)
    test_ibuf_scaled.cc
                     the scaled storage (pixel scale 2, 3, 6): oracle O1
                     (the op stream equals NN of the scale-1 reference after
                     every op; tracker_complete), get/put/blit for every pair
                     of scales, put_phys, get_pixel8, fill_static, copy()
                     out of range, wide and malformed RLE scans
    test_pngio.cc    Import_png8/Import_png32 free their buffers when libpng
                     fails half-way through a file
    rle_writer.h     test-only writer and reference decoder of RLE frame data
                     (scans of any width, malformed runs on demand)
    test_rle_writer.cc
                     its self-test against Shape_frame::encode_rle and paint_rle
    test_world_scale.cc
                     the scale policy (render_scale off/art/auto/force:N and
                     its caps), the present filter ladder and tracked_to_phys
                     (imagewin/world_scale.h)
    hires_test_util.h/.cc
                     scratch directories, a chunk-level PNG builder, a fake
                     source provider and the synthetic game of data/hires/rules
    test_hires_rules.cc
                     hashes, the T1 terrain key, the palette-0 clamp, rule P4,
                     the class-preserving reduction (shared vectors)
    test_hires_png.cc
                     the raw-index PNG reader and writer (hardening, failures)
    test_hires_store.cc
                     the override store: rules and IDs, groups, roots,
                     reduction, terrain overrides, bundles, the store set
    test_editor_fixtures.cc
                     PNGs laid out like Aseprite and GIMP exports
  present/         hires_present: data-free present tests (offscreen video
                   driver, software renderer; exit code 77 = skipped)
    test_present.cc  the read-back harness and World_presenter: 1:1 exact,
                     palette ticks, partial and clamped uploads, the 2x2 box,
                     the halving chain, palette alpha 0, letterbox bars, a
                     resize after create and the reset events (ARGB; INDEX8
                     too with SDL 3.4 or newer)
  data/
    ibuf_golden.txt  digests recorded on unmodified upstream imagewin/ibuf8.cc
    hires/           hi-res store fixtures (synthetic palette and pixels only)
      hash_vectors.txt  CRC32, FNV-1a-64, Get_color8, T1 and palette vectors,
                        shared with tools/hires (pytest)
      rules/            rule fixtures + expected.txt and the synthetic game
                        (game/palettes.flx, game/shapes.vga), shared with
                        tools/hires/hirescheck.py
      editor/           editor-like exports + expected.txt; editor/real/ (WP-15)
                        holds real exports when present
      bundle/           a pack root whose flats.bundle and pack.txt were written
                        by tools/hires/u7hires/pack.py
      make_fixtures.py, make_mkpack_bundle.py
                        the generators (deterministic)
  game/            game-data scripts for "make check-game"
    test.cfg.in      config template; lib.sh fills it in per sandbox
    lib.sh           shared helpers (skip rule, sandbox, run, log checks,
                     --render-test runs made twice with equal digests)
    smoke.sh         exult --buildmap 0 from a sandbox
    buildmap_golden.sh
                     oracle O0: --buildmap 0, 1 and 2 with render_scale=art
                     give the SHA-256 lists in golden/ (skipped in ASan builds)
    render_regions.sh
                     --render-test on the regions of regions.txt at S = 2, 3
                     and 6: NN of the scale-1 render (O2), sub-rect repaints
                     that change neither render (O6), the mini screenshot
                     (O7; not the light count, which needs a main actor and
                     the ireg objects); the present read-back at 1:1 and
                     1280x800 (ARGB and INDEX8, which must really be INDEX8
                     on SDL >= 3.4 and is skipped before), a game area with
                     offset_x 17, passes=flats
    resize_cycle.sh  the S cycle force6 -> off -> force3 -> force6 (I12), a
                     terrain edit at S > 1, the pushed resize; fails on a
                     "[hires] mixed-scale" log line
    perf.sh          the world paint at S=6 (bench=200, -O2 builds only):
                     p95 <= 2 ms at 320x200, and within 20 % of this host's
                     line in perf_baseline.json
    regen_goldens.sh records golden/*.sha256 with the reference build
    regions.txt      the map regions (tile, size, roof level)
    golden/          buildmap{0,1,2}.sha256: hash lists, never pixels
    perf_baseline.json
                     paint p95 per host ("host/case/key": ms)

Running
-------

Autotools (any build tree):

  make check          builds and runs hires_unit and hires_present
  make check-game     runs game/*.sh; set U7_BG_STATIC to the "static"
                      directory of a Black Gate installation, otherwise every
                      script is skipped (exit code 77)
  make regen-goldens UPSTREAM_BUILD=<build of upstream + P3>
                      records game/golden/*.sha256 again (U7_BG_STATIC too);
                      commit the lists with the cause in the message

The game scripts run "exult --render-test" (render_test.cc; the keys are
listed at the top of that file and in DESIGN.md section 4.2). A run writes
digest.json (FNV-1a-64 of every render, the mini screenshot digest, the
present texture formats, timings) and, on a failure, the indexed images
ref_1x.png, hi_<tag>.png and diff_<tag>.png into its sandbox, which is then kept
(KEEP_SANDBOX=1 keeps passing ones too). These images are derived from the
game's art: never commit them. For a look at a region at S=6:

  exult -c <cfg> --bg --render-test "tx=800,ty=1330,w=320,h=200,scales=6,mode=plain,out=<dir>"

perf.sh --record writes this host's line into perf_baseline.json.

make check runs the tests with SDL_VIDEO_DRIVER=offscreen,
SDL_RENDER_DRIVER=software and SDL_AUDIO_DRIVER=dummy. The configure variable
TEST_WRAPPER is prefixed to every test program; a sanitizer build sets it, for
example

  TEST_WRAPPER="env ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1 UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:print_stacktrace=1 setarch x86_64 -R timeout 900"

The game scripts prefix EXULT_WRAPPER (environment) to every exult call; see
game/lib.sh for that and the other variables. When it is empty and exult is an
ASan build, lib.sh uses

  env ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:print_stacktrace=1 setarch x86_64 -R

(ASLR off: the g++ 9.4 ASan runtime hangs at random under WSL2's ASLR; leak
checks off for the engine). The top-level make check-game builds "all" first,
and the data build runs ASan tools in a sanitizer tree, so run make there under
the same environment, for example

  env ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:print_stacktrace=1 setarch x86_64 -R timeout 5400 make check-game

Makefile.mingw (Windows, via Makefile.common), from the source directory:

  make -f Makefile.mingw check-hires     builds hires_unit.exe and
                                          hires_present.exe and runs both

hires_unit takes doctest's options, for example -tc='ibuf golden*' to run only
the test cases whose names match, or -s to list successful assertions too.
HIRES_TEST_DATA overrides the data directory compiled into it. The store tests
create scratch directories (hires_unit.<pid>.<n>) under HIRES_TEST_TMP, else in
the current directory, and remove them; one test case reads the installed
Black Gate palette when U7_BG_STATIC is set and is skipped otherwise.

The hi-res store fixtures (data/hires)
--------------------------------------

Everything in data/hires is synthetic: no palette or pixel of the game is
stored, only the CRC of Black Gate's palette 0 (checked against the installed
game when U7_BG_STATIC is set). rules/ and the crc32, fnv1a64 and get_color8
lines of hash_vectors.txt come from tools/hires/tests/make_rule_fixtures.py;
expected.txt names the primary rule (or OK) and the severity of each file, as
the engine and hirescheck.py report them. editor/ and the t1 and pal8_crc32
lines are written by make_fixtures.py (Python 3, standard library only), and
bundle/ by make_mkpack_bundle.py with the u7hires package. Rerunning the
generators gives the same bytes; change a fixture only together with both
test suites. make_rule_fixtures.py and u7hires/pack.py live on the art track
(branch hires-art), which keeps its own copy of rules/ in
tools/hires/tests/data/rules: until the two tracks merge into one copy, the
two trees must stay byte-identical.

The scale-1 golden (data/ibuf_golden.txt)
-----------------------------------------

test_ibuf_golden runs a fixed-seed stream of 24,000 ops over every
Image_buffer8 primitive on scale-1 buffers and compares the FNV-1a-64 digest
after each block of 1,000 ops with data/ibuf_golden.txt. The buffers are owned
buffers, layer buffers with a guard band and a padded pitch, and (blocks 20-23)
layer buffers with a logical offset, as the engine's main buffer has when the
full area is larger than the game area (offset_x = 17 for a 320x200 game area
in 355x200). The digests cover the whole memory of the buffers, so writes
outside the logical area count too. Its purpose is invariant I2: the scale-1
path stays byte-identical to upstream.

The file was recorded on unmodified upstream imagewin/ibuf8.cc and imagebuf.h
(upstream master 8b6ab6b43), before any hi-res change to the image buffer
code. The digests do not depend on the platform: the stream uses its own PRNG,
and fill_static (which draws from std::rand()) is checked against a model of
upstream's loop and then undone instead of being hashed.

A mismatch means that the scale-1 behaviour changed. Do not record the file
again to make the test pass. Record it only when the test itself changes (the
generator, the fixtures or the digest), and then only on unmodified upstream
image-buffer code: apply the test change to an upstream checkout, build
hires_unit there and run the recording test case, which never runs otherwise
(--no-skip) and fails without the variable:

  HIRES_IBUF_GOLDEN_RECORD=/path/to/ibuf_golden.txt hires_unit -tc='ibuf golden: record*' --no-skip

The comparing test ignores HIRES_IBUF_GOLDEN_RECORD. Each block has its own
generator, so a new block leaves the digests of the others unchanged. Commit
the new file with the reason in the commit message.

doctest
-------

doctest.h is the single-header release of doctest, unmodified:

  version   2.5.3 (tag v2.5.3, released 2026-07-06)
  source    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/doctest/doctest/v2.5.3/doctest/doctest.h
            identical to doctest/doctest.h in the release asset
            https://github.com/doctest/doctest/releases/download/v2.5.3/doctest-v2.5.3.tar.gz
  sha256    cfd518a3ef90f67e1f3ba514df23fb3627437de1a2feeba78cf5062a40021421  doctest.h
  licence   MIT (the release's LICENSE.txt, reproduced below)

To update it, replace doctest.h with a newer release header and record the new
version and checksum here.

